        {
          "id": 2985091506,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "It is an improvement in that the user should be notified that, according to\nMS-SMB 2.2.1.4.1:\nThis flag SHOULD NOT be used by the client and MUST be ignored by the\nserver unless on a named pipe as discussed in section 3.2.4.3.1 and section\n3.3.5.5.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6901460,
            "username": "cturney111",
            "public_email": "cturney111@gmail.com",
            "name": "Cal Turney",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://gitlab.com/uploads/-/system/user/avatar/6901460/avatar.png?v=1782345656",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/cturney111"
          },
          "created_at": "2026-01-06T00:29:54.799Z",
          "updated_at": "2026-01-06T00:29:54.799Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 443848487,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 23058,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 2985564926,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "MS Server does use the SYNCHRONIZE flag in its packets, regardless of what the specification says. If the text shown in Wireshark is the same for both true and false values of this flag, it's impossible to debug what MS server sends. The purpose of Wireshark is to display what is in the communication, not to judge if the communication's logic is sound. The recent change basically masks a value that can be displayed in a way that shows _maybe_ for both _true_ and _false_.",
          "author": {
            "id": 31326884,
            "username": "Dugy1",
            "public_email": null,
            "name": "Dugy1",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/cfd101187a8bdc18b7ec4335396ebd633bdafba744a1ad2a4df7461235e5b45e?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/Dugy1"
          },
          "created_at": "2026-01-06T08:50:31.272Z",
          "updated_at": "2026-01-06T08:50:31.272Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 443848487,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 23058,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b3a5b4499eff13a0593e5f7cf20ffc592325f7d1",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2987473411,
          "type": null,
          "body": "It IS possible to debug what the MS server sends by simply looking at the value of the Synchronize bit. As a protocol analyst of 30 years, I have never seen a consequence of this bit being set or reset. It is essentially a 'don't care' bit that is only meaningful for named pipes, which are typically of very low volume. For the record, stating that the synchronize bit should be ignored does not judge the communications logic is sound, it simply indicates that the client and server should disregard this bit.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6901460,
            "username": "cturney111",
            "public_email": "cturney111@gmail.com",
            "name": "Cal Turney",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://gitlab.com/uploads/-/system/user/avatar/6901460/avatar.png?v=1782345656",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/cturney111"
          },
          "created_at": "2026-01-06T23:47:48.934Z",
          "updated_at": "2026-01-06T23:47:48.934Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 443848487,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 23058,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "940d9e2dedfb4b7b40d75d7cba665ba098108ccd",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2987490329,
          "type": null,
          "body": "If someone is developing a SMB server or client and needs it to be compliant even with less common features, he needs to implement named pipes, as well as debug them and analyse the behaviour observing existing implementation. You can't handwave it as useless, it's part of the protocol and should be shown (and not just by forcing the user to impractically look at some cryptic bit in the packet's binary representation).\n\n@alagoutte , you were mentioning this problem too, right?",
          "author": {
            "id": 31326884,
            "username": "Dugy1",
            "public_email": null,
            "name": "Dugy1",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/cfd101187a8bdc18b7ec4335396ebd633bdafba744a1ad2a4df7461235e5b45e?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/Dugy1"
          },
